<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Estimating a power of a number just below 1</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>To estimate \({@Nd@}^{@n@}\) you can write \({@Nd@} = 1 + x\).</p><p>(a) What is \(x\)?</p><div>\(x =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Use the first four terms of the expansion of \((1 + x)^{@n@}\) to estimate \({@Nd@}^{@n@}\), correct to 4 decimal places.</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>A number just below 1 is \(1 + x\) with \(x\) negative. Keep the signs: odd powers of a negative number are negative and even powers are positive, so the terms alternate in sign.</p><p>(a) \({@Nd@} = 1 + ({@xd@})\), so \(x = {@xd@}\).</p><p>(b) \((1 + x)^{@n@} = 1 + {@n@}x + {@C2@}x^2 + {@C3@}x^3 + \cdots\). With \(x = {@xd@}\) the four terms are \({@tv[1]@}\), \({@tv[2]@}\), \({@tv[3]@}\) and \({@tv[4]@}\). Their sum is \({@S4d@}\), so \({@Nd@}^{@n@} \approx {@R4@}\).</p><p>A calculator gives \({@calc@}\ldots\), which also rounds to \({@R4@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[dec(v) := block([k:0], while k<10 and not(integerp(ev(10^k*v,simp))) do k:k+1, if k=0 then string(v) else dispdp(float(v),k));
pad3(r) := if r<10 then sconcat("00",r) else if r<100 then sconcat("0",r) else sconcat(r);
grp(v) := if v<0 then sconcat("-",grp(-v)) else if v<10000 then sconcat(v) else sconcat(floor(v/1000),"\\,",pad3(mod(v,1000)));
money(v) := block([c], c: floor(100*v+1/2), if mod(c,100)=0 then grp(c/100) else sconcat(grp(floor(c/100)),".",if mod(c,100)<10 then sconcat("0",mod(c,100)) else sconcat(mod(c,100))));
/* Design: truncated expansion with a negative x. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 2, Chapter 5, Exercise A, question 4(c). */
L: [[5,2/100],[7,2/100],[8,2/100],[9,2/100],[5,3/100],[7,3/100],[8,3/100],[5,4/100],[7,1/100],[9,1/100]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
n: L[ix][1];
d: L[ix][2];
N: 1-d;
xv: -d;
S4: sum(binomial(n,r)*xv^r,r,0,3);
plus: sum(binomial(n,r)*d^r,r,0,3);
allm: 1-n*d-binomial(n,2)*d^2-binomial(n,3)*d^3;
two: 1-n*d;
C2: binomial(n,2); C3: binomial(n,3);
Nd: dispdp(float(N),2);
Pd: dispdp(float(1+d),2);
dd: dispdp(float(d),2);
xd: dispdp(float(xv),2);
tv: makelist(dec(binomial(n,r)*xv^r),r,0,3);
S4d: dec(S4);
R4: dispdp(float(floor(10^4*S4+1/2)/10^4),4);
calc: dispdp(float(N^n),8);]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
